# Next.js + TypeScript

This project uses **Next.js 16 App Router**. APIs and conventions may differ from older Next.js — consult `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` and heed deprecations.

## TypeScript

- Strict mode is on. No `any`, no `@ts-ignore` without a comment justifying it.
- Prefer `type` for object shapes, `interface` for extensible contracts.
- Export named functions and types; avoid default exports except for Next.js page/layout/route files.
- Narrow at the boundary (Zod, type guards). Inside core logic, types should already be precise.

```ts
// BAD
export default function f(data: any) { return data.id; }

// GOOD
type User = { id: string; email: string };
export function getUserId(user: User): string { return user.id; }
```

## Server vs client

- Components are Server Components by default. Add `"use client"` only when you need state, effects, or browser APIs.
- Data fetching belongs in Server Components, Server Actions, or Route Handlers — not in client `useEffect`.
- Never import a `server-only` module from a Client Component.

## File and folder conventions

- App routes: `src/app/(group)/segment/page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`.
- Route Handlers: `src/app/api/.../route.ts` — export `GET`/`POST`/etc.
- Shared logic: `src/lib/<domain>/`. Shared types: `src/types/`.
- Reusable hooks: `src/hooks/`. Server Actions: `src/server/actions/`.

## Reusable code

- Extract a component or hook the second time you copy logic.
- Co-locate small helpers with their consumer; promote to `src/lib/` when reused across features.
- Keep components small (under ~150 lines) and single-purpose.

## Naming — generic SaaS only

- Never bake clinic/medical/appointment-specific terms into infrastructure or shared modules.
- Use generic SaaS vocabulary: `tenant`, `member`, `resource`, `schedule`, `booking`, `customer`.
- Bad: `clinicId`, `doctorService`, `patientList`. Good: `tenantId`, `providerService`, `customerList`.

## Errors

- Throw typed errors from server code; convert to safe responses at the boundary.
- Never swallow errors silently. If catching to recover, log with context.
